@page "/azure-monitor"

<PageTitle>Azure Monitor - bit Belemetry</PageTitle>

<h1>Azure Monitor</h1>

<p class="page-intro">
    The Azure Monitor exporter posts straight to the Application Insights ingestion endpoint
    (<code>/v2.1/track</code>), with no collector in between. Page views, requests, dependencies, exceptions, custom
    events and metrics land in their usual tables - <code>pageViews</code>, <code>requests</code>,
    <code>dependencies</code>, <code>exceptions</code>, <code>customEvents</code>, <code>customMetrics</code> - so the
    portal's Application Map, Failures, Performance and Usage blades work as they do with Microsoft's own SDKs.
</p>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Connection string</h2>
    <p>
        The exporter needs the resource's connection string: the instrumentation key and the regional
        <code>IngestionEndpoint</code> (records go to <code>{IngestionEndpoint}/v2.1/track</code>; without one, to
        <code>https://dc.services.visualstudio.com</code>), plus the <code>LiveEndpoint</code> for Live Metrics.
        Outside the browser, with <code>options.UseEnvironmentVariables</code> on, an unset connection string is read
        from <code>APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING</code> - and that variable alone adds the exporter when none
        is configured, which is what App Service and Container Apps set.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@BasicCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
    <p>
        The browser script exports to Application Insights too (<code>IncludeInBrowser</code>, on by default) - the
        same arrangement as Microsoft's JavaScript SDK, whose connection string is in every page it runs on. The
        instrumentation key identifies the resource; it does not authorize reading anything. Turn
        <code>IncludeInBrowser</code> off to keep the browser away from it, and use the
        <a href="/transport">Relay transport</a> to send browser records through .NET instead.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Microsoft Entra ID</h2>
    <p>
        A resource with local authentication disabled only accepts telemetry with an Entra ID token.
        <code>AccessTokenProvider</code> supplies one, sent as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>, for the scope
        <code>https://monitor.azure.com//.default</code>. Belemetry takes no dependency on Azure.Identity: the provider
        is a plain delegate, so any credential - managed identity, workload identity, a client secret - plugs in.
    </p>
    <p>
        An exporter with a token provider is never handed to the browser script, which could not hold the token
        safely. On Blazor Server and Hybrid, browser records reach such a resource through the Relay transport. In
        WebAssembly the .NET pipeline runs in the browser too, so it is no place for a credential either: there, send
        telemetry to a server of yours (an OpenTelemetry Collector with the Azure Monitor exporter, for example) and let
        that server authenticate.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@EntraCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>MaskIp</h2>
    <p>
        Application Insights derives each record's city and country from the client IP address it was sent from.
        With <code>MaskIp</code> (on by default) Belemetry sends <code>ai.location.ip = 0.0.0.0</code>, so no
        location is derived and the user's address is not part of the record. Belemetry never adds the client IP
        itself. Turn it off only if you need the geography and your privacy notice covers it.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@MaskIpCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Live Metrics</h2>
    <p>
        With <code>EnableLiveMetrics</code> the .NET side streams requests, dependencies, exceptions, CPU and memory to
        the portal's Live Metrics blade once per second while someone is watching it - following the Application
        Insights QuickPulse protocol, so treat it as a preview feature. It is not available in the browser, and in
        WebAssembly it reports the app running in that one browser, so it is most useful with the
        <a href="/server-pipeline">server pipeline</a> and in Hybrid apps.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@LiveMetricsCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Compression, timeout and signals</h2>
    <p>
        <code>Compression = BelemetryCompression.Gzip</code> compresses the envelope array (<code>Content-Encoding:
        gzip</code>), in the browser too. <code>Timeout</code> bounds one request (30 s). <code>Signals</code> restricts
        what is sent, by the browser script as well - for example metrics only, while traces and logs go to an OTLP
        backend. Throttling (429, 439), transient failures
        (408, 500, 502, 503, 504) and the items of a partial success (206) are retried, and kept by the
        <a href="/persistence">offline persistence</a> while the network is down. Metrics are always sent as deltas,
        which is what <code>customMetrics</code> expects, whatever <code>Metrics.Temporality</code> says.
        <code>MinimumLogLevel</code> is the per-exporter counterpart of a logging provider's level: Application
        Insights gets the plain log records at or above it while an OTLP backend gets everything, from one pipeline;
        events (page views, custom events, web vitals, exceptions), spans and metrics always pass.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@MoreCode" Language="C#" />
    <p>
        How each record becomes an envelope - the types, tags and properties - is on the
        <a href="/azure-mapping">Azure mapping</a> page.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>API</h2>
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryAzureMonitorExporterOptions" />
</div>

@code {
    private const string BasicCode = """
        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            options.ServiceName = "MyApp";            // ai.cloud.role
            options.ServiceNamespace = "Shop";        // ai.cloud.role = "[Shop]/MyApp"

            options.AddAzureMonitorExporter(builder.Configuration["ApplicationInsights:ConnectionString"]!);
        });
        """;

    private const string EntraCode = """
        using Azure.Core;
        using Azure.Identity;

        var credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();

        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            options.AddAzureMonitorExporter(builder.Configuration["ApplicationInsights:ConnectionString"]!, azure =>
            {
                azure.AccessTokenProvider = async cancellationToken =>
                    (await credential.GetTokenAsync(
                        new TokenRequestContext(["https://monitor.azure.com//.default"]), cancellationToken)).Token;
            });
        });
        """;

    private const string MaskIpCode = """
        options.AddAzureMonitorExporter(connectionString, azure =>
        {
            azure.MaskIp = true;      // the default: ai.location.ip = 0.0.0.0
        });
        """;

    private const string LiveMetricsCode = """
        options.AddAzureMonitorExporter(connectionString, azure =>
        {
            azure.EnableLiveMetrics = true;
        });

        // On an ASP.NET Core host, together with the server pipeline:
        options.Server.CollectDotnetTelemetry = true;
        """;

    private const string MoreCode = """
        options.AddAzureMonitorExporter(connectionString, azure =>
        {
            azure.Compression = BelemetryCompression.Gzip;
            azure.Timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(15);
            azure.Signals = BelemetrySignals.Metrics;
            azure.MinimumLogLevel = LogLevel.Warning;    // warnings and errors here; everything to the OTLP backend
            azure.IncludeInBrowser = false;
        });
        """;
}
